    <node id="Commission">
      <data key="d0">Commission</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Commission is an authority that the scientific panel can request documentation or information from regarding AI provider activities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission is an entity responsible for developing and recommending model contractual terms for high-risk AI systems, facilitating cooperation in the value chain.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission, commonly known as the European Commission, serves as the executive branch of the European Union (EU) and is entrusted with a wide array of critical responsibilities. It plays a pivotal role in proposing legislation, implementing decisions made by the EU Parliament and the Council, and upholding the EU treaties. The Commission works in conjunction with the Advisory Forum to ensure stakeholder representation, enhancing its regulatory effectiveness.

Among its numerous duties, the Commission focuses significantly on technology and artificial intelligence (AI). It is responsible for adopting implementing acts and codes of practice related to content regulation, ensuring compliance with obligations surrounding AI-generated content. The Commission evaluates measures taken by Member States concerning AI practices to ensure alignment with EU law, promoting the standardization of activities linked to quality management.

Additionally, the Commission oversees compliance with both national and EU regulations pertaining to AI, including the management of an EU database for high-risk AI systems. It identifies general-purpose AI models that may present systemic risks, engages with stakeholders to establish performance metrics and benchmarks, and assesses certification and compliance costs for small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs). Furthermore, it serves as the main point of contact for reporting serious incidents involving AI models, fostering a responsible AI ecosystem.

Operating under the framework of Regulation (EU) No 182/2011, the Commission enhances its decision-making capabilities and oversees penalties that Member States may impose during AI-related litigation. It also facilitates access to experts for Member States and encourages the adoption of voluntary codes of conduct related to AI systems. These efforts underline the Commission's commitment to effective governance and regulation in the evolving landscapes of AI and biometric identification systems within the EU, while also promoting AI literacy among relevant stakeholders.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission refers to the European Commission, responsible for establishing data spaces and facilitating data sharing among businesses and government entities.</data>
