    <node id="Regulation (EU) No 182/2011">
      <data key="d0">Regulation (EU) No 182/2011</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulation (EU) No 182/2011 outlines procedural rules for the EU's legislative decision-making process and serves as a legal reference in the context mentioned.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) No 182/2011 outlines the rules and principles for the control exercised by Member States over the EU Commission's implementing powers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fa5fe9ac042448be58de6c82bb33ea4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37c30ee0e0a87b8ea9630ea20f092fa</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_98.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_175.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934173</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Committee">
      <data key="d0">Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Committee is a group that assists the Commission and operates under the regulations specified in Regulation (EU) No 182/2011.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e37c30ee0e0a87b8ea9630ea20f092fa</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_98.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934103</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Parliament">
      <data key="d0">European Parliament</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The European Parliament is a crucial legislative body within the European Union (EU) that plays a significant role in the creation, amendment, and enactment of laws affecting member states across various sectors. It is directly elected to represent the interests of EU citizens and is engaged in discussions and decisions related to a broad range of policies, including technology, cybersecurity, migration, asylum, labor regulations, consumer protection, and commercial practices.

In its legislative capacity, the European Parliament collaborates closely with the Council of the EU and the European Commission to develop and oversee regulations and directives. Its actions are reflective of the concerns and needs of EU citizens, with a strong emphasis on ethical considerations in emerging technologies, such as artificial intelligence (AI). This commitment is evidenced by initiatives like the Directive (EU) 2022/2557, which addresses high-risk AI systems, as well as the significant Digital Services Act and Directive (EU) 2019/1937.

The European Parliament is responsible for proposing and approving legislation, ensuring compliance and transparency within the EU legal framework. It actively receives reports and assessments from the Commission concerning the enforcement of AI-related regulations and establishes rules governing various aspects of life in the EU, which include technology, safety, copyright, digital markets, and the regulation of medical devices and machinery.

Overall, the European Parliament's responsibilities encompass not only legislative decision-making but also budgeting and the development of directives and regulations that adequately address the complexities and needs of the EU populace. This makes it an integral part of the EU's legislative structure, representing the citizens of member states and ensuring their voices are heard in the legislative process.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Parliament is one of the legislative bodies of the European Union, involved in the creation and amendment of regulations like 2018/858 and 2024/1689.</data>
