    <node id="Stakeholders">
      <data key="d0">Stakeholders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stakeholders encompass a diverse array of groups and individuals engaged in the development, oversight, and regulation of artificial intelligence (AI) systems. This includes a wide spectrum of actors, such as business entities, civil society organizations, academia, trade unions, and standardization organizations. All parties involved or affected by AI development, including industry representatives, start-ups, small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), and the scientific community, are considered stakeholders. 

These stakeholders play critical roles in the governance of AI, contributing to the monitoring, implementation, and risk mitigation associated with AI systems. They provide valuable input that informs effective regulation and helps ensure that a variety of interests and perspectives are represented in discussions about AI policies and practices. This collaborative approach emphasizes the importance of inclusive participation from all sectors, ensuring comprehensive oversight in the evolving landscape of artificial intelligence.&lt;SEP&gt;Stakeholders encompass individuals and groups that have an interest in the regulations and include industry representatives, SMEs, and civil society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c14e1756e1d8d71ac3ba07125f122da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20ac675fffdeba0fbc242b392d3af8da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-256a6003a5841f2764536c55f7086431&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6a5cc59273abbe42c743808026477c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849f8f0403e6b12fa8a9c24b84c094c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad86065dee426d0a1db7444552c4a385</data>
