    <node id="Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012">
      <data key="d0">Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 establishes the framework for standardisation in the European Union, covering various sectors including AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 is a legislative framework established by the European Parliament and Council that outlines the procedures for standardization in the European Union. This regulation specifically sets out the requirements for the development and adoption of harmonized standards applicable to various products, including artificial intelligence (AI) systems. It governs the European standardization process, aiming to promote innovation and competitiveness within the single market. Additionally, it amends several previous directives to create a cohesive regulatory environment for European standardization. Through these provisions, Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 plays a crucial role in ensuring consistency and quality in standards across member states.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 outlines the framework for the European standardisation process including responsibilities and objectives for standardisation.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 outlines procedures regarding standards and specifications in the context of EU compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 pertains to standardization in the EU, impacting compliance measures related to AI systems and their evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3177468233ec37ad2a71dc38caa17377&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24261c10195e33e965bd7e966c33337&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2739984aae71aa27a0af867bc197b730&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee6d94a5f64203f09d02387acbdb39c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0695d12d62d4fd15a528842231db1850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43e82a7af456b2ae813a7217f92c2008&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cc9a7191827dda78eda5924876e5f7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6fc829d58f1a7bbf3defd5cbe758b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f41337092de45470ab838aff17309ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-010f171cc1a7f217689efe00fd8cb656</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_40.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_121.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_81.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_41.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Person">
      <data key="d0">Natural Person</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural person refers to an individual human being who can be held legally accountable, especially in contexts related to high-risk AI systems. This distinction is important as it separates natural persons from artificial entities, such as organizations. In various applications, particularly concerning legal and biometric identification, natural persons are significantly impacted by privacy and data protection laws. 

Natural persons also participate as subjects in the testing of AI systems under real-world conditions, providing informed consent for their involvement. Their reliability and potential risks related to criminal behavior are often evaluated using AI systems within the legal process. This highlights the importance of understanding natural persons not just as individuals but also in terms of their rights and ethical considerations in discussions surrounding AI. Overall, the term "natural person" underscores the critical role that individual human beings play in legal frameworks and technological advancements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ba8e0dd4981bd71dedb04a86b45601&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19283d37b8085eb0b86aff77d85558de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c9405f93ec3e0ccf6209ed15ef0263f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b28a4b325cc05a6cc4897f58ccdf00f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43e82a7af456b2ae813a7217f92c2008</data>
