    <node id="p. 1">
      <data key="d0">p. 1</data>
      <data key="d1">reference</data>
      <data key="d2">p. 1 indicates a specific page or section, likely referring to the location of information in a document or report.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4b7c7b85b3435f5f316dd1ac957764</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_80.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934239</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SMEs">
      <data key="d0">SMEs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SMEs (Small and Medium-sized Enterprises) are a category of businesses that have specific interests and needs regarding the regulation of AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Small and Medium-sized Enterprises (SMEs) are crucial components of the economy, recognized for their significant contributions as providers or deployers of artificial intelligence (AI) systems. Defined by having a limited number of employees and specific turnover thresholds, SMEs encompass businesses that often include start-ups. Due to their relatively constrained resources, SMEs are frequently the focus of regulatory frameworks aimed at fostering innovation and promoting economic growth.

To support SMEs in meeting these challenges, they benefit from free access to AI regulatory sandboxes, which provide a controlled environment for testing and developing AI technologies. These sandboxes are designed to facilitate innovation while minimizing the regulatory burdens that SMEs face. Additionally, SMEs are encouraged to participate in the development of standards in accordance with EU regulations, highlighting their importance as stakeholders in the regulatory process.

When navigating compliance with various regulations, SMEs are able to provide technical documentation in a simplified manner, which addresses specific certification and compliance costs they encounter. This approach aims to alleviate the economic strain on these enterprises, which may otherwise face potential fines or regulatory pressures related to their operations in the AI sector. Overall, SMEs play a vital role in shaping the landscape of AI innovation, and regulatory guidelines increasingly consider their unique challenges and contributions.&lt;SEP&gt;Small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) play a vital role in the advisory forum, representing commercial interests and innovation.&lt;SEP&gt;SMEs, or Small and Medium-sized Enterprises, benefit from simplified compliance methods that aim to reduce costs associated with regulatory obligations concerning AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Small and Medium-sized Enterprises (SMEs) are included as part of the commercial interests represented in the advisory forum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6e7b8a0eb1cb8c27dfd822da15429870&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b235644744fa597c94328afbeaa7e56b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e76c1f88e5810a7e79cf9527486831e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aca56f82b70892874784bcec5c2bb2c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-712b56cbfc73c46eacde9204fa4e5c49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24261c10195e33e965bd7e966c33337&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36cb0fd5abf1b169a8aa13021f58c7b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8f21b63428a38759e45ffcb10bac3d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0271020749ae70ce56cbd607a6f6662a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad86065dee426d0a1db7444552c4a385&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7365f713d09e74d49090cb78cb3e19b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65c79a1c0cb37c10df906970d376029d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40f3761e589fb7702938fd4fbbecfdcc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_109.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_99.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_58.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_143.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_121.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_150.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_62.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_8.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_96.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_67.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_11.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_95.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936275</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Start-ups">
      <data key="d0">Start-ups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Start-ups are a subset of small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) characterized by their innovative nature and a focus on technology, particularly in the area of artificial intelligence (AI). These newly established businesses often aim to create innovative products or services and are typically in the early stages of development. As part of efforts to support their growth and compliance, start-ups have access to AI regulatory sandboxes, which provide them with free resources to test and navigate various regulatory requirements related to AI deployment. Moreover, start-ups play a crucial role in the innovation landscape and are represented among various stakeholders in advisory forums dedicated to addressing regulatory issues. Their need for support in navigating these regulations is essential for fostering their development and ensuring successful integration of their innovations into the market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95424b9dff57302628c496570b4baff4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e76c1f88e5810a7e79cf9527486831e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0271020749ae70ce56cbd607a6f6662a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_143.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_139.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_150.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_62.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Public Authorities">
      <data key="d0">Local Public Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local Public Authorities are entities involved in the communications with SMEs and start-ups to assist them regarding the implementation of the Regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Local Public Authorities are mentioned as stakeholders in the development of guidelines to ensure their needs are addressed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e76c1f88e5810a7e79cf9527486831e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b235644744fa597c94328afbeaa7e56b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_96.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_62.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934354</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conformity Assessment Fees">
      <data key="d0">Conformity Assessment Fees</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Conformity Assessment Fees are charges assessed for the evaluation of compliance with regulations, significant for SMEs.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformity Assessment Fees are the costs associated with ensuring compliance with the Regulation, which may be reduced for SMEs based on their size and market situation.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformity Assessment Fees relate to costs that SMEs incur for ensuring their AI systems comply with regulatory requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e76c1f88e5810a7e79cf9527486831e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-712b56cbfc73c46eacde9204fa4e5c49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_62.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_143.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934332</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Activities">
      <data key="d0">Training Activities</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Training Activities refer to specific programs organized to raise awareness and educate SMEs and start-ups about the application of the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e76c1f88e5810a7e79cf9527486831e5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_62.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934244</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standardisation Development Process">
      <data key="d0">Standardisation Development Process</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Standardisation Development Process is the procedure by which SMEs and relevant stakeholders participate in the formulation of standards related to AI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e76c1f88e5810a7e79cf9527486831e5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_62.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934244</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Operators">
      <data key="d0">Operators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Operators are individuals or organizations responsible for deploying and managing AI systems, subject to compliance with regulations and obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Operators are the entities or individuals that will utilize AI systems and are affected by the obligations arising from the Regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Operators encompass individuals or entities involved in the AI value chain, including importers and distributors, responsible for various roles and obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Operators refer to entities or individuals managing or overseeing the implementation and functioning of high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e3d60cf6cbe0ae91a9793cb5c98a4a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-197c7357cf5fefe6ca4e08f847ff6a75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c168b1207668ad4ba646371f91bfce09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0bd7056742f766183c11176d91fb1e8</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_83.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_124.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_62.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Information Platform">
      <data key="d0">Information Platform</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Information Platform is a centralized resource developed by the AI Office to provide accessible information regarding the Regulation to all relevant operators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e3d60cf6cbe0ae91a9793cb5c98a4a92</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_62.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934244</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standardised Templates">
      <data key="d0">Standardised Templates</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standardised Templates are pre-defined documents provided by the Commission to assist SMEs and start-ups in meeting regulatory requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Standardised Templates are pre-set documents intended to help SMEs navigate regulation compliance upon request.&lt;SEP&gt;Standardised templates are predefined formats created by the AI Office to facilitate compliance with the Regulation, ensuring uniformity for operators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-712b56cbfc73c46eacde9204fa4e5c49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3d60cf6cbe0ae91a9793cb5c98a4a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_62.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_143.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934332</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EU Database">
      <data key="d0">EU Database</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The EU Database is a centralized and secure repository established by the European Commission to register and track high-risk AI systems in accordance with European Union regulations. It plays a crucial role in ensuring compliance with product standards and provides essential support for regulatory processes. This database not only documents the registration of AI systems but also facilitates the management and regulation of data related to law enforcement practices across Member States.

Under specific regulations outlined in the EU legal framework, providers are mandated to register high-risk AI systems within the EU Database. This repository contains detailed records and specifications, including sections and annexes that pertain to the relevant legal articles, ensuring comprehensive oversight and adherence to compliance in the deployment of AI technologies within the Union.

Furthermore, the EU Database serves as an informational repository monitored by regulatory authorities, where data regarding the compliance and evaluation of AI systems is stored. It is also identified in regulations as a source of information for market surveillance authorities to check compliance concerning AI systems. Overall, the EU Database is an official system instrumental in regulatory compliance and data management related to high-risk AI technologies within the European Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7f74259ac0ec20e9f3989df868f56b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a64bfb94635a0df2f808b2f8a494bd3e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4672fd3df7077626af67d20e46d3fcb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610573a7c194ad780ed97782200a740c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb5ca2a2f12bb39286916d398b70ed9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa9e2916955e882c73231ed69851f6ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72498ec90c19419f10373dde7e7b51b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00e7606a0fa9d588f18d67d02cfcf654&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78dcdae0f5d22df28405d8ae03e038d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f116a95954c59b63ce3149e6283ed8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_60.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_83.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_49.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_71.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_53.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_80.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936108</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Compliance">
      <data key="d0">Non-Compliance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Compliance refers to the failure to meet regulatory requirements, which can trigger actions by the market surveillance authority against providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ddb5ca2a2f12bb39286916d398b70ed9</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_83.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934245</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Risk AI System Compliance">
      <data key="d0">High-Risk AI System Compliance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Risk AI System Compliance involves the legal and regulatory processes ensuring that AI systems categorized as high-risk meet strict safety and operational standards.&lt;SEP&gt;High-risk AI system compliance refers to the adherence to regulatory standards and corrective actions necessary when a system does not conform to these standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e978e96ac6d99cf448b53260c07dded&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb5ca2a2f12bb39286916d398b70ed9</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_83.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_20.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934461</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Market Surveillance Authorities">
      <data key="d0">National Market Surveillance Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">National Market Surveillance Authorities are responsible for monitoring market activities to ensure compliance with laws and regulations related to product safety and consumer protection.&lt;SEP&gt;National Market Surveillance Authorities are responsible for supervising AI systems at the national level, particularly in cases where the AI system is not high-risk or does not involve overlapping competences with the AI Office.&lt;SEP&gt;National market surveillance authorities monitor compliance with laws and regulations regarding market practices in Member States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b90abf0636c26d546e9b6724e3224ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_161.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934555</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compliance Evaluations">
      <data key="d0">Compliance Evaluations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Compliance Evaluations are assessments conducted to ensure that AI systems meet established legal and regulatory standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Compliance evaluations are assessments conducted to determine if AI systems adhere to the regulatory obligations required for high-risk classifications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4ac0ccf9bd954306cee6efbfd342416&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b90abf0636c26d546e9b6724e3224ae</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_75.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_161.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936210</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cross-Border Cases">
      <data key="d0">Cross-Border Cases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cross-Border Cases pertain to regulatory situations that involve multiple jurisdictions and require cooperation among authorities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b90abf0636c26d546e9b6724e3224ae</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_161.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934248</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutatis Mutandis">
      <data key="d0">Mutatis Mutandis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutatis mutandis refers to a principle allowing for modifications in certain contexts while preserving the original meaning or application.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4f5323e3b01efa8f64e095d14892512c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_161.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934248</data>
    </node>
    <node id="legal acts">
      <data key="d0">legal acts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Legal acts are formal legislation or regulations established to govern compliance assessments and procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-55bfb9287798720e37fcd8bae961e88b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_11.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934249</data>
    </node>
    <node id="technical progress">
      <data key="d0">technical progress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Technical progress refers to advancements or improvements in technology that may necessitate changes in documentation and compliance requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-55bfb9287798720e37fcd8bae961e88b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_11.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934249</data>
    </node>
    <node id="scientific panel">
      <data key="d0">scientific panel</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The scientific panel consists of experts that Member States can call upon for support in enforcing regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The scientific panel is a group of independent experts established to provide expertise and support for the implementation of AI regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The scientific panel is an independent group of experts established under Article 68, tasked with advising the Commission on the classification of AI models based on their systemic risk and compliance with regulations. Comprised of knowledgeable professionals, the panel performs assessments and evaluations of general-purpose AI models, ensuring impartiality and confidentiality throughout its tasks. In addition to offering insights, the scientific panel is responsible for issuing alerts regarding potential risks posed by AI models and supporting the effective implementation of AI regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The scientific panel supports governance systems for AI and monitors risks associated with general-purpose AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec7c9ce58b650afe456517c2483e56c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ea6a588c10ad2fe493ec8cc1913f72d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a0c1908573a7d6a832a6b35befbd7ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbcc9707fb1611da88a5526a5558a165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-140dff8b8263721e25ea86a4104c273d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6579442a2f027e643cf907006333003a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_163.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_69.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_113.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_151.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_90.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936304</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 91">
      <data key="d0">Article 91</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 91 is a provision within the regulatory framework that relates to the compliance requirements for providers of general-purpose AI models regarding document submission.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 91 outlines obligations for AI model providers regarding compliance evaluations and information requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b16c22014fb75795531a9a187b8fc81</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_101.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_92.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935914</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 90">
      <data key="d0">Article 90</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 90 outlines the obligations and responsibilities regarding the identification of systemic risks associated with AI models at the Union level.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 90 provides details on the procedures and criteria for alerts about risks associated with AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 90 refers to a policy or guideline regarding the development and regulation of general-purpose AI models within a legal framework.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935174</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 68">
      <data key="d0">Article 68</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 68 discusses the standards for appointing independent experts for evaluations of AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934254</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 101">
      <data key="d0">Article 101</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 101 is noted in the Regulation as an exception to the rules detailed in other chapters, indicating a different timeline for application.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 101 outlines the penalties for providing incorrect, incomplete, or misleading information in the context of AI model compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 101 sets forth the penalties and fines related to non-compliance and failure to provide access to AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e93fbd1f7fc7cbb860b708f989987b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c553a0db4b39033e7fcfeddeb912aac</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_113.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_91.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_92.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Independent Experts">
      <data key="d0">Independent Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Independent Experts are individuals selected to evaluate AI models, ensuring unbiased assessment and adherence to legal standards in AI implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts are professionals or academics who provide objective insights and recommendations in the regulation process.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts are individuals engaged by the AI Office to conduct evaluations and ensure compliance with the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-075ecb5835777e6044264a23ca0cb20a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fcd3af92cad1d85ebf561f059acb114&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_164.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936121</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Internal Testing">
      <data key="d0">Internal Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Internal Testing refers to the procedures and assessments conducted within the provider's organization to ensure the AI model's functionality and safety prior to external evaluations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-075ecb5835777e6044264a23ca0cb20a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934254</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Risks">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Risks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic Risks refer to the potential hazards and vulnerabilities associated with the implementation and deployment of General-Purpose AI Models. These risks have the capacity to significantly impact society, markets, and broader systems, thereby necessitating regulatory oversight and careful management. Key areas of concern include public health, safety, and societal structures, where the adverse effects of such AI models may manifest.

The concept of Systemic Risks encompasses various threats that can emerge from the design and functioning of AI systems, which may lead to serious incidents and widespread negative consequences across multiple stakeholders. As a result, there is a pronounced need for comprehensive identification, assessment, and management of these risks, as well as the establishment of a robust regulatory framework to recognize and address potential hazards effectively. Overall, Systemic Risks highlight the importance of vigilance and proactive measures in the effective governance of AI technologies to safeguard against their inherent dangers.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risks refer to the potential widespread impacts and threats associated with failures in AI systems, which the AI Office must monitor and mitigate.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risks are potential threats posed by AI models that could lead to significant societal or economic impacts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9cac929d0048fe34377179e8ecde4a5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-161f3120961644fbdc17c7b09b46e54b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fb0ee5d200ca5b71eb6d9f53ad070ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c49e78c25e30ed3a2557915010f14f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e23bb4c71b510fec0bbe6570de37cd5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1ef1c9ca92c002cf6ad36264fe1292c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-447d51c65bac6d28fd62c36c3e70e08b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-075ecb5835777e6044264a23ca0cb20a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fcd3af92cad1d85ebf561f059acb114&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3f46abe104a1bdb0fae1c25094db36d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bee8a141261944c2193bcb00b304b768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b5f24963810305f2a7846d54923bf0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_53.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_114.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_164.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_112.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_115.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_110.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_116.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_118.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_52.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936312</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evaluations">
      <data key="d0">Evaluations</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Evaluations refer to the formal processes undertaken to assess the compliance and safety of the general-purpose AI models, involving various stakeholders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-075ecb5835777e6044264a23ca0cb20a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934254</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union or Member State law">
      <data key="d0">Union or Member State law</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Union or Member State law provides the legal framework under which biometric data processing for law enforcement can be authorized and regulated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-737358b61293029e1a6d7326160654fa</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_94.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934255</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 10">
      <data key="d0">Article 10</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 10 of Directive (EU) 2016/680 pertains to the safeguards required for the processing of personal data.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 10 of Directive (EU) 2016/680 specifies conditions under which biometric data processing is necessary for law enforcement, emphasizing the need for appropriate safeguards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a46250ea72037444e96653239f2893b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-737358b61293029e1a6d7326160654fa</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_140.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_94.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 4 (1)">
      <data key="d0">Article 4 (1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 4 (1) of Directive (EU) 2016/680 outlines principles such as lawfulness, fairness, and transparency that must be respected during the processing of biometric data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-737358b61293029e1a6d7326160654fa</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_94.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934255</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Ecosystem">
      <data key="d0">AI Ecosystem</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The AI Ecosystem refers to the interconnected environment in which AI technologies are developed, implemented, and regulated, involving various stakeholders and authorities.&lt;SEP&gt;The AI Ecosystem refers to the network of actors involved in the development, implementation, and regulation of artificial intelligence technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475967c83e624bc711a9ff13e8150163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulatory Framework">
      <data key="d0">Regulatory Framework</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulatory Framework refers to the overarching set of laws and regulations governing the development and use of artificial intelligence systems within the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;The Regulatory Framework encompasses the laws and regulations governing the establishment and operation of AI regulatory sandboxes.&lt;SEP&gt;The Regulatory Framework outlines the rules and guidelines governing the establishment and operation of AI sandboxes, ensuring responsible innovation.&lt;SEP&gt;The regulatory framework refers to the comprehensive set of laws and guidelines governing the deployment and utilization of AI technologies and systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The regulatory framework establishes the rules and guidelines for the operation, reporting, and oversight of AI systems in the EU.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d08b537f9a5bb2b4f4bbdf442ba39bdd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475967c83e624bc711a9ff13e8150163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0c31436f0e7ce27ce5952aa2bf9ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a84d660603389dd91dcd96134d5bb46e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b89491b02af0a00cbc13f9e8df827a6d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_73.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_57.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_103.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935057</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Regulatory Sandboxes">
      <data key="d0">AI Regulatory Sandboxes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI Regulatory Sandboxes are controlled environments designed to foster innovation by supporting the development, training, testing, and validation of AI systems prior to their entry into the market. These initiatives enable companies, particularly small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) and start-ups, to test innovative AI systems in a secure setting while ensuring compliance with relevant regulations. Established by national competent authorities, AI Regulatory Sandboxes provide a structured framework for the experimentation of AI technologies under regulatory supervision. They allow for testing in real-world conditions, alleviating legal and administrative burdens, and promoting competitiveness within the industry. Overall, AI Regulatory Sandboxes play a crucial role in facilitating the responsible advancement of artificial intelligence by balancing innovation with regulatory compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;AI Regulatory Sandboxes are frameworks within which AI technologies can be tested in real-world conditions under regulatory oversight.&lt;SEP&gt;AI Regulatory Sandboxes are controlled environments designed to foster innovation in artificial intelligence by allowing developers to test and experiment with AI systems while ensuring compliance with regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;AI Regulatory Sandboxes refer to controlled environments established to allow for the testing and development of artificial intelligence systems while ensuring compliance with relevant regulations and standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95424b9dff57302628c496570b4baff4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d08b537f9a5bb2b4f4bbdf442ba39bdd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aca56f82b70892874784bcec5c2bb2c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7859a0c655f74761757b416c3b50bf36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ffd8df9d8eb9625dca23bc5f974e025&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98532c431ec75ae38c76dc0730337a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0271020749ae70ce56cbd607a6f6662a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_143.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_139.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_25.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_57.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union and National Law">
      <data key="d0">Union and National Law</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Union and National Law refers to the legal frameworks that AI regulatory sandboxes must comply with to ensure safety, rights, and market standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d08b537f9a5bb2b4f4bbdf442ba39bdd</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exit Reports">
      <data key="d0">Exit Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Exit Reports are documents produced at the conclusion of sandbox activities, detailing the compliance, results, and learnings of AI system tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Exit Reports are documents that demonstrate compliance with regulatory standards, used in the context of AI regulatory sandboxes to validate testing outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d08b537f9a5bb2b4f4bbdf442ba39bdd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Protection Authorities">
      <data key="d0">Data Protection Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Data Protection Authorities are regulatory bodies that ensure the protection of personal data and privacy rights within their jurisdictions in relation to AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Data Protection Authorities oversee the enforcement of data protection laws and ensure that personal data is handled in accordance with regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Data Protection Authorities are specific public bodies that oversee and ensure compliance with data protection regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2315cd7abb88dee440f081de5430a32f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_57.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_157.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Competitiveness and Innovation">
      <data key="d0">Competitiveness and Innovation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Competitiveness and Innovation are key goals aimed at fostering the development of AI ecosystems and ensuring that new technologies thrive in the market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934267</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conformity Assessment Process">
      <data key="d0">Conformity Assessment Process</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Conformity Assessment Process is a systematic examination to ensure that a product meets relevant regulations and standards, particularly in relation to the AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934267</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commission and the Board">
      <data key="d0">Commission and the Board</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Commission and the Board are regulatory bodies responsible for the oversight and governance of AI systems within the framework of European Union regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cross-Border Cooperation">
      <data key="d0">Cross-Border Cooperation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cross-Border Cooperation refers to collaborative efforts between different national competent authorities to facilitate the regulation and supervision of AI systems across borders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-184360d53ae652d7f48a919e168f375a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Data Protection Authorities">
      <data key="d0">National Data Protection Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">National Data Protection Authorities are regulatory bodies responsible for overseeing the processing of personal data and ensuring compliance with data protection laws.&lt;SEP&gt;National data protection authorities oversee the enforcement of data protection laws and make sure that personal data is handled correctly in Member States.&lt;SEP&gt;National Data Protection Authorities are responsible for enforcing data protection laws and have the power to issue prohibition decisions on biometric data processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184360d53ae652d7f48a919e168f375a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc038db2f25d511bf84cccc9550e10c2</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_39.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936283</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitigation of Risks">
      <data key="d0">Mitigation of Risks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitigation of Risks refers to measures taken to reduce significant risks to health, safety, and fundamental rights identified during the development of AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-184360d53ae652d7f48a919e168f375a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Regulatory Sandbox Project">
      <data key="d0">AI Regulatory Sandbox Project</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The AI Regulatory Sandbox Project is an initiative designed for testing innovative AI applications in a controlled regulatory environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-184360d53ae652d7f48a919e168f375a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Administrative Fines">
      <data key="d0">Administrative Fines</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Administrative fines are financial penalties imposed by authorities, particularly by the European Data Protection Supervisor (EDPS), for non-compliance with various regulations. These fines are specifically applied to organizations that violate data protection laws and can vary in amount depending on the nature of the offense. According to Article 99(1) of the AI Regulation, Member States may also impose administrative fines for violations related to AI experimentation. The imposition of these penalties is governed by specific rules and considerations, which outline the application process for addressing regulatory infringements. Administrative fines serve as a mechanism for public authorities to enforce legal compliance within Member States and promote adherence to established regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7365f713d09e74d49090cb78cb3e19b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-828a2d9070cae319b764e3b609bc9b81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c31e49eb2274702ec6ced9550f1ca86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184360d53ae652d7f48a919e168f375a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-599d61ad7be34e2603c3fef13f2ff8dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de5a0c7f560be7dcbe4301aed20bcb98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5da2f6b1c2fa5619efa26719534e9c35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-111c71f234a0f2d1b26490907acae6eb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_112.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_99.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_57.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_100.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supervisory Powers">
      <data key="d0">Supervisory Powers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supervisory Powers pertain to the authority granted to competent authorities to oversee, regulate, and enforce compliance during the operation of AI regulatory sandboxes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-184360d53ae652d7f48a919e168f375a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual Reports">
      <data key="d0">Annual Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Annual Reports are documents submitted by deployers of high-risk AI systems detailing their use in law enforcement, required by regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Annual Reports are documents that deployers of high-risk AI systems must submit to regulatory authorities detailing their usage, compliance, and impacts of such systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Annual reports are documents submitted by national competent authorities that outline the progress and findings related to AI sandboxes.&lt;SEP&gt;Annual reports refer to the documents published by the Commission detailing the use and efficacy of remote biometric identification systems in publicly accessible spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4988a00689afb1245e0f0ce4ceef5921&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85bb41c1db2d2e1883172e505d5f7b71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4882abf1dd869d3dff00b8d88ee946a8</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Specific Plan">
      <data key="d0">Specific Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific plan refers to the detailed guidelines and protocols that must be followed by providers participating in the AI sandbox.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85bb41c1db2d2e1883172e505d5f7b71</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guidance">
      <data key="d0">Guidance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Guidance refers to the recommendations and instructions given by competent authorities to ensure compliance with regulatory frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85bb41c1db2d2e1883172e505d5f7b71</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support">
      <data key="d0">Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support encompasses the assistance and resources provided by the AI Office to national competent authorities for effective sandbox operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85bb41c1db2d2e1883172e505d5f7b71</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compliance">
      <data key="d0">Compliance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Compliance indicates the act of adhering to specific laws and regulations during AI experimentation and implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Compliance involves adhering to laws and regulations governing data processing and protection, especially for high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85bb41c1db2d2e1883172e505d5f7b71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0639a0bd62e8b0bbe41bc1bee4a3098</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_10.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934516</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Incidents">
      <data key="d0">Incidents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Incidents refer to any issues or problems that arise during the implementation of AI systems in the regulatory sandbox.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85bb41c1db2d2e1883172e505d5f7b71</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Best Practices">
      <data key="d0">Best Practices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Best practices are guidelines and recommendations established to ensure effective operation and implementation of AI technologies within the regulatory sandbox.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6adb64ff4e7b459fa1df815eac98b096</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reports">
      <data key="d0">Reports</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reports are documents that provide detailed information on the progress, results, and recommendations regarding the implementation of AI regulatory sandboxes over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6adb64ff4e7b459fa1df815eac98b096</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934270</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Analytic Tools">
      <data key="d0">Risk Analytic Tools</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk analytic tools are methods or systems used to evaluate potential risks involving individuals or entities, often in the context of financial or security assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4274f38f6b4aeff6dc2959e9e1a87f1a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_42.json</data>
