      <data key="d5">1783934909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General-Purpose AI Models Providers">
      <data key="d0">General-Purpose AI Models Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Providers of general-purpose AI models are invited to contribute to the creation of codes of practice, ensuring that their operations align with regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Articles 53 and 55">
      <data key="d0">Articles 53 and 55</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Articles 53 and 55 refer to specific provisions in the regulation that outline obligations for AI practices and codes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Approaches">
      <data key="d0">International Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">International approaches refer to best practices and standards developed globally for the regulation and use of AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;International approaches refer to the global standards and practices that the AI Office considers while drawing up codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;International approaches signify collaborative efforts among nations to address the ethical and practical concerns surrounding the development and deployment of AI technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3f46abe104a1bdb0fae1c25094db36d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b5f24963810305f2a7846d54923bf0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_116.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_110.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Codes of Practice">
      <data key="d0">Codes of Practice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Codes of practice are guidelines designed to ensure ethical and safe development and use of AI models, taking into account diverse perspectives and international standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of practice are guidelines developed at the Union level to manage the obligations and ensure the safe use of AI technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of practice are guidelines established to ensure ethical and safe use of AI technologies by providers, aimed at mitigating risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of practice function as guidelines for providers of AI models to demonstrate compliance with the obligations set forth in the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b5f24963810305f2a7846d54923bf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c8a6b92fbe8d93eb878957c5ed60aa0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_117.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_116.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936000</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Civil Society Organizations">
      <data key="d0">Civil Society Organizations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Civil society organizations are groups that advocate for public interests and provide diverse perspectives in the development of codes for AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil society organizations are non-governmental entities that represent the interests of the public and may contribute to the development of AI regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b5f24963810305f2a7846d54923bf0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_116.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="codes of practice">
      <data key="d0">codes of practice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Codes of practice are guidelines developed to facilitate compliance with obligations related to AI-generated or manipulated content.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of practice are regulatory frameworks that set specific objectives, commitments, and performance indicators for stakeholders involved in AI development and implementation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50cb89ed7d0d818018d373ebefa65267&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_50.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936149</data>
    </node>
    <node id="general-purpose AI models">
      <data key="d0">general-purpose AI models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">General-purpose AI models are types of artificial intelligence designed for a wide range of tasks and may be subject to regulatory scrutiny.&lt;SEP&gt;General-purpose AI models are versatile AI constructs that can be employed across various applications and sectors.&lt;SEP&gt;General-purpose AI models are versatile artificial intelligence systems designed to perform a wide range of tasks and can be adapted for various applications. These models are intended for broad usage and are subject to oversight under specific regulations. Providers of general-purpose AI models have obligations regarding their technical documentation, which ensures transparency and accountability. Due to their high-impact capabilities, these models present associated systemic risks that need to be managed effectively. The evaluation and oversight of these models are conducted by entities such as the AI Office and a scientific panel, reflecting their importance and the need for responsible governance in their deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;General-purpose AI models refer to AI models that can be used for various purposes and are subject to specific regulatory compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;General-purpose AI models refer to AI systems that can perform a wide variety of tasks and are subject to specific governance and monitoring activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a0c1908573a7d6a832a6b35befbd7ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b16c22014fb75795531a9a187b8fc81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4dc89de2edca5d2bfe0d5e9e7c99665b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2739984aae71aa27a0af867bc197b730&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af991f85166d6ddfbbf84e4a73207d3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4ac0ccf9bd954306cee6efbfd342416</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_163.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_40.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_75.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_162.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_53.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_101.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936304</data>
    </node>
    <node id="civil society organizations">
      <data key="d0">civil society organizations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Civil society organizations are various groups representing the interests of individuals and communities, contributing to the development of codes of practice and advocacy for stakeholder needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="industry">
      <data key="d0">industry</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The industry includes businesses and organizations involved in developing, deploying, and utilizing general-purpose AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;The industry represents businesses and enterprises that engage in AI development and application, playing a critical role in adhering to codes of practice and regulatory measures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="academia">
      <data key="d0">academia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Academia refers to educational institutions and researchers involved in studying and setting standards for AI, influencing the codes of practice through research and expertise.&lt;SEP&gt;Academia represents educational institutions that are involved in research and development relevant to AI and data exchange.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a811acaf13b6420a717f6aa21a6ed331&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_165.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936190</data>
    </node>
    <node id="downstream providers">
      <data key="d0">downstream providers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Downstream providers are entities that use AI systems developed by others, needing to align their practices with established codes of practice for safety and compliance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="independent experts">
      <data key="d0">independent experts</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Independent experts are individuals with up-to-date scientific or technical expertise in the field of AI, selected to provide objective assessments and recommendations.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts are professionals with no vested interests, brought in to provide objective assessments regarding the impact of AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts are specialists or authorities in the field of AI who provide insights and evaluations about the effectiveness and safety of AI practices in relation to the codes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6579442a2f027e643cf907006333003a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee04f6104a24650416810ede28f3790b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_151.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_96.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935690</data>
    </node>
    <node id="affected persons">
      <data key="d0">affected persons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Affected persons are individuals who may be impacted by AI technologies and practices, whose needs and interests must be considered in the development of codes of practice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="key performance indicators">
      <data key="d0">key performance indicators</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Key performance indicators are measurable values that demonstrate how effectively organizations are achieving their objectives set in the codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Key performance indicators are measurable values used to assess the achievement of objectives and effectiveness of initiatives in various contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a811acaf13b6420a717f6aa21a6ed331&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_165.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936190</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 98(2)">
      <data key="d0">Article 98(2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 98(2) provides a comprehensive framework for the examination procedure involved in adopting implementing acts concerning obligations related to artificial intelligence (AI) standards. It outlines specific guidelines governed by EU law that the Commission must follow when making decisions, particularly in relation to the examination of implementing acts associated with the regulation of AI systems and notified bodies. This article serves as a critical provision within the relevant legislation, delineating the procedural steps required for the effective implementation of these obligations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de6a0d59bb86cca6093fbb52cf156b4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3034aa8ff1b6d41ac6e0b8ff655b76ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff61ec794793cf787d4a0473487a06f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97182f6b8815d40f51faf3e8eaeb7c7d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_60.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_50.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_37.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_101.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936238</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2 May 2025">
      <data key="d0">2 May 2025</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">2 May 2025 is the deadline by which the codes of practice for AI model providers are expected to be ready.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2 August 2025">
      <data key="d0">2 August 2025</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">2 August 2025 is the date by which the AI Office must finalize the code of practice or assess its adequacy.&lt;SEP&gt;2 August 2025 is the deadline for Member States to communication the identity of testing and market surveillance authorities and publish necessary contacts.&lt;SEP&gt;2 August 2025 is the effective date for the implementation of the provisions on penalties within the Regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;2 August 2025 marks the deadline by which providers must comply with specific regulatory obligations regarding AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22275ba6343521309923fef233affa2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ad9752391e53e840a2d8ac0861f8948&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ce3f90343791af10ffae2a7848f26e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_70.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_179.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 98">
      <data key="d0">Article 98</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 98 refers to a specific section within a legal or regulatory document that outlines procedures for implementing acts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bec338e77f01240f23cac5480d2c8ae4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783934443</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 2">
      <data key="d0">Article 2</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 2 outlines the specific issues that must be addressed in accordance with legal or regulatory frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bec338e77f01240f23cac5480d2c8ae4</data>
