    <node id="scientific community">
      <data key="d0">scientific community</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The scientific community encompasses researchers and experts who contribute knowledge and methodologies for assessing AI models' capabilities and risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="civil society">
      <data key="d0">civil society</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Civil society refers to the organizations and institutions that represent the interests and will of the public in discussions regarding AI regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="systemic risks">
      <data key="d0">systemic risks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic risks involve the potential for significant negative consequences arising from the capabilities of general-purpose AI models, particularly their impact on markets and society.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risks refer to potential negative consequences that could arise from the widespread use of AI systems, affecting societal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7570653f66cb58454b80be2046705a29</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_120.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935668</data>
    </node>
    <node id="technical tools and methodologies">
      <data key="d0">technical tools and methodologies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Technical tools and methodologies are frameworks and processes used to evaluate the capabilities and risks associated with general-purpose AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935120</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cumulative computation">
      <data key="d0">cumulative computation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cumulative computation refers to the total amount of computation, measured in floating-point operations, used in training AI models, which helps assess their capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935120</data>
    </node>
    <node id="threshold of floating point operations">
      <data key="d0">threshold of floating point operations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The threshold of floating point operations is a predetermined level of computational effort that general-purpose AI models must meet to qualify as having systemic risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935120</data>
    </node>
    <node id="training data set">
      <data key="d0">training data set</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A training data set comprises the data used to train AI models, which influences their performance and capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935125</data>
    </node>
    <node id="end users">
      <data key="d0">end users</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">End users refer to individuals or organizations that interact with and utilize the outputs of general-purpose AI models in various applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935125</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scientific Community">
      <data key="d0">Scientific Community</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Scientific Community comprises researchers and experts from various disciplines who contribute knowledge relevant to the assessment and development of AI technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90efb8f063f236753e4794005afa2a0d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Industry">
      <data key="d0">Industry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Industry represents commercial stakeholders in the advisory forum, influencing regulation and standards development.&lt;SEP&gt;The Industry refers to companies and organizations that develop and implement AI technologies, playing a critical role in shaping AI capabilities and standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90efb8f063f236753e4794005afa2a0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_150.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936275</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Civil Society">
      <data key="d0">Civil Society</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Civil Society includes non-governmental organizations, activists, and communities involved in discussions about the ethical implications and risks associated with AI technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil society includes social partners and is involved in the stakeholder representation within the advisory forum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90efb8f063f236753e4794005afa2a0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_150.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thresholds">
      <data key="d0">Thresholds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thresholds are predetermined levels or criteria used to evaluate the capabilities and risks associated with AI models, guiding regulatory assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90efb8f063f236753e4794005afa2a0d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Benchmarks">
      <data key="d0">Benchmarks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Benchmarks are standards or reference points against which the performance and impact of AI models can be assessed over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90efb8f063f236753e4794005afa2a0d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935127</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Publicly Accessible Space">
      <data key="d0">Publicly Accessible Space</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Publicly Accessible Space refers to any physical area that can be accessed by an undetermined number of individuals, regardless of ownership or intended use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commerce">
      <data key="d0">Commerce</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Commerce includes spaces like shops and restaurants where goods and services are exchanged.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Services">
      <data key="d0">Services</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Services include areas like banks and hospitality venues that provide essential services to the public.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sport">
      <data key="d0">Sport</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sport encompasses facilities like swimming pools, gyms, and stadiums intended for athletic activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transport">
      <data key="d0">Transport</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transport includes spaces like bus, metro, and railway stations, as well as airports designed for transit of people.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Entertainment">
      <data key="d0">Entertainment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Entertainment refers to spaces such as cinemas, theatres, and museums where recreational activities occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leisure">
      <data key="d0">Leisure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leisure spaces include public roads, parks, and playgrounds meant for relaxation and recreational activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Security Restrictions">
      <data key="d0">Security Restrictions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Security Restrictions pertain to limitations imposed on access to certain spaces for public safety reasons.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Privately Owned Space">
      <data key="d0">Privately Owned Space</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Privately Owned Space includes areas that are restricted to specific individuals or groups, typically representing personal property.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935132</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Space">
      <data key="d0">Physical Space</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical Space is a general term for any tangible area that can be occupied or accessed by individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capacity or Security Restrictions">
      <data key="d0">Capacity or Security Restrictions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Capacity or Security Restrictions dictate the maximum allowable number of individuals in a space or impose specific entry requirements for safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19ac689968d1b5ce1db014f781096bd3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Company and Factory Premises">
      <data key="d0">Company and Factory Premises</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Company and factory premises are defined as workplaces intended for access only by relevant employees and service providers, thus not publicly accessible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prisons">
      <data key="d0">Prisons</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Prisons are facilities designed to confine individuals who have been convicted of crimes and are not considered publicly accessible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Private Residential Building">
      <data key="d0">Private Residential Building</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A private residential building can include spaces that are both publicly accessible and non-publicly accessible, such as hallways leading to specific apartments or offices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airport">
      <data key="d0">Airport</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Airports are crucial transport hubs; however, they contain both publicly accessible areas and restricted zones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Spaces">
      <data key="d0">Online Spaces</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Online spaces refer to digital environments that are not considered physical and therefore are not subject to the same accessibility rules as physical spaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unlockable Door">
      <data key="d0">Unlockable Door</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">An unlockable door represents a physical barrier that can be accessed when unlatched, though it may not imply public access due to surrounding conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open Gate">
      <data key="d0">Open Gate</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">An open gate signifies a potential access point which still may not indicate public accessibility depending on surrounding indications or regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Workplaces">
      <data key="d0">Workplaces</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Workplaces refer to locations such as offices or factories specifically designed for employee activities and access is likely restricted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Relevant Employees">
      <data key="d0">Relevant Employees</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Relevant employees are the individuals authorized to access certain restricted spaces such as offices and factories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935136</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Data Protection Board">
      <data key="d0">European Data Protection Board</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The European Data Protection Board is an independent European body that ensures consistency in the application of data protection law across the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Data Protection Board is an independent European body that ensures the consistent application of data protection rules across the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Data Protection Board oversees the enforcement of data protection regulations within the European Union, ensuring compliance with privacy laws.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Data Protection Board is responsible for ensuring consistent application of data protection laws in the EU and advising on policy matters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae8bdbb57af24d78fe2313201571bea7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1b92a727425c41637e16da3cb9ac9c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85605aee8aaac2bf4ad29d80d902148f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-472b03a027e3fcae21e86e23849e4510</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_10.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_180.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_3.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="18 June 2021">
      <data key="d0">18 June 2021</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">18 June 2021 is the date when the joint opinion of the European Data Protection Supervisor and the European Data Protection Board was delivered.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-472b03a027e3fcae21e86e23849e4510</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_180.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935141</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 42(1) and (2)">
      <data key="d0">Article 42(1) and (2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 42(1) and (2) of Regulation (EU) 2018/1725 outlines the consultation procedure for data protection authorities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-472b03a027e3fcae21e86e23849e4510</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_180.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935141</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulation (EU) 2019/881">
      <data key="d0">Regulation (EU) 2019/881</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulation (EU) 2019/881 is a legislative framework established by the European Parliament and Council aimed at enhancing cybersecurity measures within the EU, particularly regarding AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) 2019/881 pertains to ENISA and establishes frameworks for cybersecurity and AI systems in the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) 2019/881 sets out requirements and measures on data governance and cybersecurity for high-risk AI systems in the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) 2019/881 specifies requirements for cybersecurity in AI systems, establishing a framework for certification and conformity statements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60a12c037b4dee54159c11f348ff3f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d423f15a7cbd742a57795f884434b60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ec0d2d3cbe2a53a3bbf6373c775edf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07b79b1855840c81b5d9079a82c7e9fb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_42.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_78.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_122.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935901</data>
    </node>
    <node id="data governance">
      <data key="d0">data governance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data governance is the management of data integrity, security, and availability, especially in the context of high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07b79b1855840c81b5d9079a82c7e9fb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_122.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935149</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cybersecurity scheme">
      <data key="d0">cybersecurity scheme</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cybersecurity schemes involve protocols and certifications that ensure compliance with cybersecurity requirements for various systems including high-risk AI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07b79b1855840c81b5d9079a82c7e9fb</data>
