      <data key="d3">chunk-3ded1c99cfd152513a4ef07b96a9c5de</data>
      <data key="d2">The declaration aims to align the Union's digital policies with human rights and fundamental freedoms.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_7.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935574</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 9(1) of Regulation (EU) 2016/679">
      <data key="d0">Article 9(1) of Regulation (EU) 2016/679</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 9(1) of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 prohibits the processing of biometric data except under limited conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 9(1) pertains to the protection of sensitive data under EU law, impacting how biometric data is categorized and used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b65e9562dd645622f1390ba1863fee19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc038db2f25d511bf84cccc9550e10c2</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_54.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_39.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936283</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Remote Biometric Identification Systems">
      <data key="d0">Remote Biometric Identification Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Remote biometric identification systems are technologies designed to identify individuals from a distance, raising high-risk considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b65e9562dd645622f1390ba1863fee19</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_54.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935578</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bias and Discrimination">
      <data key="d0">Bias and Discrimination</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bias and discrimination refer to the potential negative impacts of inaccurate biometric identification on various demographic groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b65e9562dd645622f1390ba1863fee19</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_54.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935578</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biometric Verification Systems">
      <data key="d0">Biometric Verification Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biometric verification systems are AI applications specifically designed to confirm a person’s identity based on their biometric attributes, as opposed to identifying them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b65e9562dd645622f1390ba1863fee19</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_54.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935578</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Discriminatory Effects">
      <data key="d0">Discriminatory Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Discriminatory effects are negative impacts stemming from biased biometric identification practices, potentially leading to unfair treatment of certain groups.&lt;SEP&gt;Discriminatory effects refer to negative outcomes that arise from biased algorithmic decisions, particularly towards marginalized groups based on age, ethnicity, race, sex, or disabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ab148daacc5693b15ec462635a5502a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65e9562dd645622f1390ba1863fee19</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_32.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_54.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936263</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Artificial Intelligence systems">
      <data key="d0">Artificial Intelligence systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Artificial Intelligence systems are advanced computational frameworks that perform tasks requiring human-like cognition and are governed by regulatory standards in the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;Artificial Intelligence systems refer to software and hardware capable of performing tasks that typically require human intelligence, particularly those categorized as safety components under Regulation (EU) 2024/1689.&lt;SEP&gt;Artificial Intelligence systems are advanced computational systems that can perform tasks typically requiring human intelligence, such as learning, reasoning, and problem-solving.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4219a2a10d85ddd82f98b8d6267cb9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7805654ce0fe0d8d9b6ba008562d2aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b46b9cdd1723562953df9638581250</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_106.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_108.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 57">
      <data key="d0">Article 57</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 57 is a specific section within Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 outlining requirements and provisions for the regulation of Artificial Intelligence systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 57 of Regulation (EU) 2018/1139 details the requirements for implementing acts concerning the safety components of Artificial Intelligence systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4219a2a10d85ddd82f98b8d6267cb9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7805654ce0fe0d8d9b6ba008562d2aa</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_108.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783935589</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 58">
      <data key="d0">Article 58</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 58 is another section within Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 that discusses the adoption of delegated acts concerning safety components of Artificial Intelligence systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 58 of Regulation (EU) 2018/1139 sets forth guidelines for the adoption of delegated acts concerning Artificial Intelligence systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 58 outlines the conditions and powers granted to market surveillance authorities regarding AI systems within a regulatory sandbox.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4219a2a10d85ddd82f98b8d6267cb9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7805654ce0fe0d8d9b6ba008562d2aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43a1516ad8d48077af0d59fc01578f33</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_76.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_108.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783936287</data>
    </node>
