    <edge source="Commission" target="market surveillance authority">
      <data key="d6">25.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Market surveillance authorities are required to submit reports to the Commission regarding the use of biometric identification systems, showing a direct line of accountability.&lt;SEP&gt;The market surveillance authority is required to inform the Commission regarding non-compliance findings of AI systems in the market.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission collaborates with market surveillance authorities to enforce compliance with AI regulations across Member States.</data>
      <data key="d8">compliance,compliance monitoring,oversight,regulatory compliance,regulatory coordination,regulatory oversight</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ee6d94a5f64203f09d02387acbdb39c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad9c740a10a5c1577e578a593bd61fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2187eb3476528ac7c75b571eb3844f0e</data>
      <data key="d10">./cached_docs/article_79.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_36.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_81.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783935926</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Commission" target="Member States">
      <data key="d6">241.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Commission and Member States engage in a collaborative and structured partnership aimed at regulating high-risk AI systems and ensuring compliance with relevant EU laws and standards. Member States play a critical role in this relationship, as they are required to notify the Commission about changes in the designation and compliance of notified bodies. This includes communication about their authority identities and the measures they enforce, particularly in relation to technologies like biometric identification systems and artificial intelligence.

Together, the Commission and Member States work to enhance AI literacy initiatives, fostering a supportive regulatory environment that prioritizes safety standards and facilitates business operations, particularly for small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) and start-ups. The Commission serves not only as a coordinator for notifications and compliance checks concerning conformity assessment bodies but also as a key player in streamlining regulations to aid businesses in navigating compliance efficiently.

Moreover, the Commission is responsible for publishing annual reports that are based on data submitted by Member States regarding biometric identification systems, indicating an ongoing exchange of information and mutual consultation. This collaboration is essential for addressing objections and authorizing regulatory frameworks that are responsive to market needs.

In essence, the cooperative effort between the Commission and Member States is designed to maintain a transparent regulatory environment, ensuring that standards and regulations are upheld consistently while also benefitting the broader business landscape. This partnership fosters an adaptive regulatory framework capable of responding to the complexities of new technological advancements while safeguarding safety and compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission works with Member States to implement funding programmes that advance the objectives of EU regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission collaborates with Member States to ensure effective implementation and enforcement of AI regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission facilitates the access of Member States to experts for support in implementing the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d8">collaboration,communication,compliance coordination,compliance efforts,compliance enforcement,compliance oversight,consultation,consultative process,cooperation,coordination,data reporting,decision-making,expert collaboration,governance,governance support,government coordination,guideline management,implementation,information sharing,inter-institutional communication,legal notification,organizational relationship,oversight,policy collaboration,policy implementation,regulation,regulatory collaboration,regulatory compliance,regulatory cooperation,regulatory enforcement,regulatory implementation,regulatory oversight,regulatory practices,reporting</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-75ec68339f2da8ef06976767a0d5b494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b837dd750c95d041180fa3d735328672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-712b56cbfc73c46eacde9204fa4e5c49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0789803641ff590acdf82b97e7d4afbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-599d61ad7be34e2603c3fef13f2ff8dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4c7e75432b8ffa8e0b96687dc4dd380&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2806e288e0b71013209c4ad85ecaa38b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c742eff7a2b610bb01c8c0ec233b62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7365f713d09e74d49090cb78cb3e19b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f94df6da4de89cee24b1bd8f504c377&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad9c740a10a5c1577e578a593bd61fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9317b056c2a260423c79e368903cde7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-595952fa3e7e47cb312adaaad303b73c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21acb22d994b8f65ea3709a99eb85032&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22275ba6343521309923fef233affa2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6001fd4ce94d732804de396c3240c65f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ea6a588c10ad2fe493ec8cc1913f72d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f79a9fca1518856b24fdcb7e59f040&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ee5867d3c03f24001b650ad752686d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345a8ba89ed0ea3325462384d5e16aaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e853663a3f2d06a9cfea8582e10f089&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86aff1d72a3f6226d0b5db29d9910a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-244204be180c4ed9731f434ed67ee99b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98532c431ec75ae38c76dc0730337a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8caee37338a6383c30fe06ea2b3e4761&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba662546e18e6e5f82c3cd1c5b4c3b75</data>
