    <edge source="AI Systems" target="Regulation">
      <data key="d6">87.0</data>
      <data key="d7">AI systems are governed by regulations that establish their operational framework and objectives.&lt;SEP&gt;The Regulation establishes a comprehensive framework governing AI systems, particularly focusing on high-risk applications. It mandates transparency requirements for these systems to ensure that deployers can operate them responsibly. Specifically, AI systems must comply with the Regulation when utilized for civilian or law enforcement purposes, thereby ensuring proper oversight of their applications.

Additionally, the Regulation outlines specific provisions for AI systems developed for scientific research and development, indicating that some of these systems could be excluded from its constraints. It also delineates conditions under which AI systems may function without posing significant risks, thereby framing guidelines for their usage. 

Furthermore, the Regulation provides crucial guidelines regarding the application and limitations of AI systems within the European Union, particularly concerning issues related to national security. Overall, AI systems are governed by this Regulation, which details requirements and standards for their development and deployment, as well as guidelines and criteria for evaluation, risk assessment, and compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Regulation specifically governs the use of AI Systems in law enforcement for biometric data processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulations are created to govern how AI systems should be designed and implemented to prevent risks and protect fundamental rights.</data>
      <data key="d8">AI governance,AI systems,compliance,governance,legal framework,operational guidelines,operational limitations,oversight,regulation compliance,regulatory framework,risk assessment,risk management,technology regulation</data>
